\f0\fs24 \cf2 As soon as you enter the passage of the labyrinth\'85 look up!\
The ceiling above you is not just a simple decoration, but a refined tribute to one of the most fascinating places of the Renaissance: the famous \'93Stanza del Labirinto\'94 in the Palazzo Ducale of Mantua. The Bagatti Valsecchi brothers didn\'92t just draw inspiration, they studied, designed, and reproduced it. In the museum's archive, there\'92s a beautiful pastel drawing that shows the care with which they reinterpreted that masterpiece. Every detail is a declaration of love for art and history.\
In the original ceiling of Mantua, at the center, there\'92s an enigmatic phrase: 
\f1\i \'93Forse che s\'ec, forse che no\'94
\f0\i0  (\'93Maybe yes, maybe no\'94). A motto taken from a 1504 love song, but also a perfect metaphor for the labyrinth of life, full of choices, doubts, and paths to follow. The Bagatti Valsecchi brothers, however, chose to draw inspiration from more moral and universal values. The phrase that stands out immediately?\
\'93
\f2\b Sic itur per angusta ad augusta
\f0\b0 \'94 \'96 
\f1\i \'93Thus, one reaches great things through difficulties.\'94\

\f0\i0 A powerful message, engraved into the ceiling as an invitation not to give up: even the most winding paths can lead to something extraordinary. And it doesn\'92t stop there. This is just the beginning of the game: in every corner of the house, other Latin phrases are hidden, small messages to decipher, like traces left between the folds of time. Can you find them all?}